		<a href="#section-authentication-methods" id="section-authentication-methods" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
		<h2>Choosing SMTP encryption and authentication</h2>

		<section>
			<div>
				<p class="wide">
				  Check your relay's setup instructions before choosing a strategy. For a submission port that requires a STARTTLS upgrade, choose <code>SMTP_TLS</code>. For a port that starts with TLS, choose <code>SMTPS</code>. The default <code>SMTP</code> tries STARTTLS when offered and allows plaintext if it isn't offered, even when you supply a password.
				</p>
				<p class="wide">
				  Set the strategy when you build the Mailer, alongside the host, port and credentials.
				</p>
<pre><code class="language-java">Mailer mailer = SimpleJavaMail.fromDefaults().mailerBuilder()
    .withSMTPServer("smtp.example.com", 587, username, password)
    .withTransportStrategy(TransportStrategy.SMTP_TLS)
    .buildMailer();</code></pre>
				<p class="wide">You can also select the strategy in your configuration.</p>
<pre><code class="language-properties small">simplejavamail.transportstrategy=SMTP_TLS</code></pre>
				<div class="doc-table-wrap" tabindex="0" aria-label="Scrollable SMTP transport strategy comparison">
					<table class="security-route-table">
						<thead><tr><th>Strategy</th><th>TLS behavior</th><th>When STARTTLS is absent</th><th>Default port</th></tr></thead>
						<tbody>
							<tr><th><code>SMTP</code> (default)</th><td>Try STARTTLS when offered</td><td>May continue in plaintext, including password authentication</td><td>25</td></tr>
							<tr><th><code>SMTP_TLS</code></th><td>Require STARTTLS before authentication or mail</td><td>Fail the connection</td><td>587</td></tr>
							<tr><th><code>SMTPS</code></th><td>TLS from the start of the connection</td><td>Not applicable; no STARTTLS upgrade</td><td>465</td></tr>
							<tr><th><code>SMTP_OAUTH2</code></th><td>Require STARTTLS before sending the access token</td><td>Fail the connection</td><td>587</td></tr>
							<tr><th><code>SMTPS_OAUTH2</code></th><td>TLS from the start, then XOAUTH2 authentication</td><td>Not applicable; no STARTTLS upgrade</td><td>465</td></tr>
						</tbody>
					</table>
				</div>
				<ul>
				  <li><strong>Check extra properties.</strong> <code>buildMailer()</code> rejects
				    <code>mail.smtp.starttls.required=false</code> with <code>SMTP_TLS</code> or <code>SMTP_OAUTH2</code>. If you
				    supply <code>mail.transport.protocol</code> with <code>SMTPS_OAUTH2</code>, keep it set to <code>smtps</code>.
				    Conflicts fail before connections or workers start.</li>
				  <li><strong>Check who configures the connection.</strong> These checks apply when Simple Java Mail creates the
				    Session, including logging-only mode. If you supply a Session or <code>CustomMailer</code>, configure the
				    connection yourself. Keep the Session stable in use and make sure custom providers and socket factories perform
				    the certificate checks you need.</li>
				  <li><strong>Check the connection you intend to use.</strong> A <a
				    href="debugging.html#section-smtp-capabilities">connection probe</a> describes its own connection. Set the
				    required behavior on the Mailer that will send the message. Choose <code>SMTP</code> only where opportunistic
				    encryption is acceptable.</li>
				</ul>
			</div>
		</section>


		<a href="#section-transport-strategy-smtp" id="section-transport-strategy-smtp" class="section-link h3">&sect;</a>
		<h3>TransportStrategy.SMTP</h3>

		<section>
<pre><code>SimpleJavaMail.fromDefaults().mailerBuilder()
    .withTransportStrategy(TransportStrategy.SMTP);</code></pre>
			<div>
				<p class="wide">
					This is the default strategy for both unauthenticated relays and username/password connections. With opportunistic TLS enabled,
					it attempts a TLS upgrade when the server advertises STARTTLS. If the server does not advertise it, the connection can continue
					in plaintext. Password authentication is not protected in that case; base64 encoding is not encryption.
				</p>
			</div>
		</section>
		<section>
			<div>
				<ul>
				  <li><strong>The server offers STARTTLS.</strong> Simple Java Mail checks the certificate chain against the JVM
				    trust store and verifies the host name when TLS is negotiated. Reserve certificate exceptions for controlled
				    test or compatibility environments.</li>
				  <li><strong>The server refuses the upgrade or TLS checks fail.</strong> The connection fails. Plaintext fallback
				    applies only when STARTTLS wasn't offered.</li>
				  <li><strong>Encryption is required.</strong> Choose <code>SMTP_TLS</code> or <code>SMTPS</code> so a missing
				    STARTTLS advertisement cannot leave you sending in plaintext. Opportunistic SMTP depends on an advertisement
				    that can be suppressed before certificate checks begin.</li>
				</ul>
			</div>
		</section>
		<section>
			<div class="view">
				<p>
					Plain SMTP attempts an optional STARTTLS upgrade by default. For a server that breaks during STARTTLS negotiation, you can turn that attempt off
					programmatically or with
					<code class="inline language-properties">simplejavamail.opportunistic.tls</code>.
					This escape hatch applies only to <code class="inline language-java">TransportStrategy.SMTP</code>. It does not change mandatory TLS,
					OAuth2 transport, or SMTPS.
					Turning it off deliberately skips STARTTLS even when offered; it is not a fallback after a failed handshake.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="side">
<pre><code class="language-java">SimpleJavaMail.fromDefaults().mailerBuilder()
	  .withTransportStrategy(TransportStrategy.SMTP)
	  .withTlsConfig(TlsConfig.builder()
	      .withOpportunisticTLS(false)
	      .build());
</code></pre>

Or with properties:
<pre><code class="language-properties small">simplejavamail.opportunistic.tls=false
</code></pre>
			</div>
		</section>


		<a href="#section-transport-strategy-smtps" id="section-transport-strategy-smtps" class="section-link h3">&sect;</a>
		<h3>TransportStrategy.SMTPS</h3>

		<section>
			<p class="wide">
				SMTP entirely encapsulated by TLS. Commonly known as SMTPS.
			</p>
<pre><code>SimpleJavaMail.fromDefaults().mailerBuilder()
    .withTransportStrategy(TransportStrategy.SMTPS);</code></pre>

			<p class="wide">
				Strict certificate validation is the default. Unless you configure an explicit exception, server certificates must be issued
			</p>
			<ol class="indent">
				<li>by a certificate authority in the JVM trust store; and</li>
				<li>to a subject matching the identity of the remote SMTP server.</li>
			</ol>
		</section>


		<a href="#section-transport-strategy-tls" id="section-transport-strategy-tls" class="section-link h3">&sect;</a>
		<h3>TransportStrategy.SMTP_TLS</h3>

		<section>
			<p class="wide">
				The connection starts with SMTP and must upgrade to TLS before authentication or mail submission. A username and password work here too;
				there is no fallback to plaintext if the server lacks STARTTLS.
			</p>
<pre><code>SimpleJavaMail.fromDefaults().mailerBuilder()
	.withSMTPServer("smtp.example.com", 587, "username", "password")
    .withTransportStrategy(TransportStrategy.SMTP_TLS);</code></pre>

			<p class="wide">
				Strict certificate validation is the default. Unless you configure an explicit exception, server certificates must be issued
			</p>
			<ol class="indent">
				<li>by a certificate authority in the JVM trust store; and</li>
				<li>to a subject matching the identity of the remote SMTP server.</li>
			</ol>
		</section>
		<a href="#section-transport-strategy-oauth2" id="section-transport-strategy-oauth2" class="section-link h3">&sect;</a>
		<h3>TransportStrategy.SMTP_OAUTH2</h3>

		<section>
			<p class="wide">
				<code class="inline language-java">SMTP_OAUTH2</code> uses normal SMTP submission but authenticates with the
				<code class="inline language-text">XOAUTH2</code> mechanism instead of a mailbox password. It uses port 587 by default and requires STARTTLS,
				so the connection is encrypted before authentication.
			</p>
			<p class="wide">
				The SMTP username identifies the mailbox and the credential is an OAuth2 access token, not a refresh token, client secret, or authorization
				code. If your application already has a token whose lifetime covers the Mailer, pass it in the usual password position.
			</p>
<pre class="full-width-example"><code class="language-java">Mailer mailer = SimpleJavaMail.fromDefaults().mailerBuilder()
    .withSMTPServer("smtp.example.com", 587, "user@example.com", accessToken)
    .withTransportStrategy(TransportStrategy.SMTP_OAUTH2)
    .buildMailer();</code></pre>
			<p class="wide">
				Simple Java Mail configures SMTP and XOAUTH2, but it does not run the OAuth authorization flow itself. Your application remains responsible
				for obtaining the access token from its provider. If the Mailer outlives that token, <a href="#oauth2-token-refresh">register a token provider</a>.
			</p>
		</section>
		<a href="#section-transport-strategy-smtps-oauth2" id="section-transport-strategy-smtps-oauth2" class="section-link h3">&sect;</a>
		<h3>TransportStrategy.SMTPS_OAUTH2</h3>
		<section>
			<p class="wide">
				Use <code>SMTPS_OAUTH2</code> when the SMTP service requires XOAUTH2 over implicit TLS. The connection is encrypted from its
				first byte, uses port 465 when no port is supplied, and does not negotiate STARTTLS. Certificate-chain and server-identity
				validation follow the same defaults as <code>SMTPS</code>. Supply a fixed token as below or <a href="#oauth2-token-refresh">register a token provider</a> for refresh.
			</p>
<pre class="full-width-example"><code class="language-java">Mailer mailer = SimpleJavaMail.fromDefaults().mailerBuilder()
    .withSMTPServer("smtp.example.com", 465, "user@example.com", accessToken)
    .withTransportStrategy(TransportStrategy.SMTPS_OAUTH2)
    .buildMailer();</code></pre>
			<p class="wide">
Select the same strategy through properties:
</p>
<pre><code class="language-properties">simplejavamail.transportstrategy=SMTPS_OAUTH2
simplejavamail.smtp.host=smtp.example.com
simplejavamail.smtp.username=user@example.com
# Fixed-token route only; omit this property when registering a provider in Java.
simplejavamail.smtp.password=your-access-token</code></pre>
		</section>
  <h3 id="oauth2-token-refresh">Refresh OAuth2 tokens on long-running Mailers</h3>
  <section><div class="wide">
    <p>If your worker runs all day and its token expires in an hour, register a token provider.
      Both OAuth2 strategies can call your existing token service when a physical connection opens or reconnects.</p>
<pre class="full-width-example"><code class="language-java">OAuth2AccessTokenProvider accessTokens =
    () -&gt; tokenService.currentAccessToken();

Mailer mailer = SimpleJavaMail.fromDefaults().mailerBuilder()
    .withSMTPServer("smtp.example.com", 587, "user@example.com")
    .withTransportStrategy(TransportStrategy.SMTP_OAUTH2)
    .withOAuth2AccessTokenProvider(accessTokens)
    .buildMailer();</code></pre>
    <ul>
      <li>Use port 465 and <code>SMTPS_OAUTH2</code> for the same callback over implicit TLS. An open pooled connection can be reused without a fresh callback.</li>
      <li>Choose one credential source per Mailer. Make the callback thread-safe and cache valid tokens.
        A provider error or a blank or null token stops the connection without exposing the token. SMTP authentication rejection reaches the caller without an automatic retry.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>In Spring, register an <code>OAuth2AccessTokenProvider</code> bean or adapt Spring Security’s OAuth2 client manager.
      See <a href="/spring.html#section-spring-oauth2">Spring integration</a>.</p>
<details class="doc-disclosure">
  <summary>Protocol, username and token choices for a supplied Session</summary>
<p class="wide" id="supplied-session-oauth2-mechanisms">
Set <code>mail.transport.protocol</code> on the Session or Mailer builder and include <code>XOAUTH2</code>
in that protocol's <code>auth.mechanisms</code> property. See the
<a href="/understanding-configuration.html#supplied-sessions">protocol settings and conflict rules</a>.
</p>
<pre class="full-width-example"><code class="language-java">// The previous Mailer using this Session has completed close().
try (Mailer reused = mail.mailerBuilder(session)
        .withProperty("mail.transport.protocol", "smtp")
        .withProperty("mail.smtp.auth.mechanisms", "XOAUTH2") // for an smtp Session
        .withOAuth2AccessTokenProvider(accessTokens)
        .buildMailer()) {
    reused.sync().sendMail(email);
}</code></pre>
			<p class="wide" id="supplied-session-oauth2-usernames">
For SMTP or STARTTLS, set <code>mail.smtp.user</code>; for SMTPS, set <code>mail.smtps.user</code>.
Direct and pooled connections use the username for the selected protocol. A username for the other protocol has no effect.
Jakarta Mail also accepts a transport-URL username and the <code>mail.user</code> fallback.
</p>
			<p class="wide" id="supplied-session-oauth2-registration">
Wait for the previous Mailer to shut down before changing a Session's credentials and building its replacement.
You can then choose a fixed token, another token provider or ordinary authentication, with matching authentication and TLS settings.
See the <a href="migration-notes-10.0.0.html#pooled-oauth-registration">fixed-token Session example</a>.
</p>
			<p class="wide" id="supplied-session-oauth2-token">
For a supplied Session, put a fixed token in the String property <code>simplejavamail.oauth2.token</code>;
inherited String defaults in <code>Properties</code> are accepted. Configure the protocol, username and XOAUTH2 mechanism as above.
Sending, connection tests and authenticated probes use that token. See the
<a href="/understanding-configuration.html#supplied-sessions">supplied-Session rules</a>.
</p>
</details>
  </div></section>

<a href="#section-gmail" id="section-gmail" class="section-link h3">&sect;</a>
  {{#> components/feature-heading-h3 core=true}}Send through Gmail{{/components/feature-heading-h3}}

  <section>
    <div class="view">
      <p>
        Gmail's <a href="https://developers.google.com/workspace/gmail/imap/imap-smtp">SMTP submission service</a> uses port 587 with STARTTLS
        or port 465 with TLS from the start.
      </p>
      <p>These examples use an <a href="https://support.google.com/accounts/answer/185833?hl=en">app password</a>, if your account supports one.
        For OAuth2, use the <a href="#section-transport-strategy-oauth2">token configuration above</a>.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="side">
<pre><code>SimpleJavaMail.fromDefaults().mailerBuilder()
  .withSMTPServer("smtp.gmail.com", 587, "you@gmail.com", "app password")
  .withTransportStrategy(TransportStrategy.SMTP_TLS);
// or:
SimpleJavaMail.fromDefaults().mailerBuilder()
  .withSMTPServer("smtp.gmail.com", 465, "you@gmail.com", "app password")
  .withTransportStrategy(TransportStrategy.SMTPS);
</code></pre>
    </div>
  </section>

		<a href="#section-requiretls" id="section-requiretls" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
		<h2>Require TLS for onward delivery</h2>

		<section>
			<div>
				<p class="wide">
				  Say a sensitive report can go through several relays after your submission server. Your Mailer's <code>TransportStrategy</code> controls only the first connection. Ask for RFC 8689 REQUIRETLS on the Email when later relays also need to use TLS.
				</p>
			</div>
<pre class="full-width-example"><code class="language-java">Email email = mail.emailBuilder().startingBlank()
    .from("sender@example.org")
    .withRecipients(mail.recipientBuilder().withName("Recipient").withAddress("recipient@example.net").withType(Message.RecipientType.TO).build())
    .withSubject("Sensitive report")
    .withPlainText("...")
    .withSmtpDeliveryOptions(SmtpDeliveryOptions.builder()
        .withTlsRequiredForOnwardDelivery()
        .build())
    .buildEmail();

MailSubmissionReceipt receipt = mailer.sync().sendMail(email);
if (receipt.isRequireTlsUsed()) {
    audit.record("REQUIRETLS was issued for this SMTP attempt");
}</code></pre>
			<div>
				<p class="wide">
					The bundled Angus adapter checks the actual send connection immediately before submission. It requires TLS, server-identity checking, a trusted
					certificate policy, and a post-TLS REQUIRETLS advertisement. If one is missing, no <code class="inline language-text">MAIL FROM</code> command is
					sent. For example:
				</p>
<pre><code class="language-text small">This email requires REQUIRETLS, but the SMTP server does not advertise usable REQUIRETLS support after TLS negotiation.
Use a server that supports RFC 8689, or clear the requirement for this email. No message was submitted.</code></pre>
				<p class="wide">
					This check is intentionally separate from a <a href="debugging.html#section-smtp-capabilities">connection probe</a>, which uses another connection.
					<code class="inline language-java">SMTP_TLS</code> and <code class="inline language-java">SMTPS</code> do not enable REQUIRETLS automatically.
					The default opportunistic <code class="inline language-java">SMTP</code> strategy can satisfy it when STARTTLS actually succeeds and all checks pass.
				</p>
			</div>
		</section>
		<section>
			<div class="view">
				<p>
					To apply the same suppressible default through property files, environment variables or Spring configuration, set:
				</p>
<pre><code class="language-properties small">simplejavamail.defaults.requiretls=true</code></pre>
				<p>
				  Clear a choice you set on <code>SmtpDeliveryOptions.Builder</code> with <code>clearTlsRequiredForOnwardDelivery()</code>. Ordinary defaults or overrides can still supply the value. Use the suppression options shown here to skip either source for one composed Email. On an exact EML builder, use the same delivery options to request REQUIRETLS while keeping the original bytes. Exact mode skips Email templates.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="side">
<pre><code class="language-java">Email email = mail.emailBuilder().startingBlank()
    .withTemplateOptions(EmailTemplateOptions.builder()
        .dontApplyDefaultValueFor(
            EmailProperty.TLS_REQUIRED_FOR_ONWARD_DELIVERY)
        .dontApplyOverrideValueFor(
            EmailProperty.TLS_REQUIRED_FOR_ONWARD_DELIVERY)
        .build())
    // compose the email
    .buildEmail();</code></pre>
			</div>
		</section>
		<section>
			<p class="wide">
			  Use <code class="inline language-java">isRequireTlsUsed()</code> to record whether the SMTP attempt sent the REQUIRETLS option. The value can be true even if the server rejects <code class="inline language-text">MAIL FROM</code>. Server acceptance, later relay behavior and final delivery are separate facts. Use <a href="#section-sending-smime">S/MIME</a> or <a href="#section-sending-openpgp">OpenPGP/MIME</a> when the message content itself needs cryptographic protection.
			</p>
		</section>


		<a href="#section-custom-ssl-factory" id="section-custom-ssl-factory" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
		<h2>Configure your own SSL connection factory</h2>

		<section>
			<div class="view">
				<p>
				  Your internal relay may require a client certificate. Supply an SSL connection factory for that connection, and keep its certificate checks in the factory.
				</p>
				<ul>
				  <li><strong>Know what fails.</strong> With a managed Angus Session, a custom factory failure stops the connection
				    by default. If the failure happens while upgrading an open connection, Simple Java Mail closes it. Your factory
				    still owns trust checks and cancellation behavior. A supplied Session retains its provider's behavior.</li>
				  <li><strong>Choose an instance or class.</strong> A class-based factory needs a public static
				    <code>getDefault()</code> method. In a modular application, export its package to
				    <code>org.simplejavamail.mailprovider.angus</code> or pass an instance. The factory is initialized lazily.</li>
				</ul>
			</div>
			<div class="side">
<pre><code>SimpleJavaMail.fromDefaults().mailerBuilder()
	.withTlsConfig(TlsConfig.builder()
	    .withCustomSSLFactoryClass(theClassName) // or:
	    .withCustomSSLFactoryInstance(theInstance) // takes precedence
	    .build())
	.buildMailer();
</code></pre>
				Or with property default:
<pre><code class="language-properties small">simplejavamail.custom.sslfactory.class=your.project.YourSSLSocketFactory</code></pre>
			</div>
		</section>
		<section>
			<div class="wide">
				<h3>Explicitly allow the standard factory as a fallback</h3>
				<p>
Set the Angus fallback property only if a connection without your custom factory's behavior is acceptable.
For <code>SMTP</code> and <code>SMTP_TLS</code>:
</p>
<pre><code class="language-java">mailerBuilder.withProperty("mail.smtp.socketFactory.fallback", "true");</code></pre>
				<p>Or in your Simple Java Mail properties file or Spring configuration:</p>
<pre><code class="language-properties">simplejavamail.extraproperties.mail.smtp.socketFactory.fallback=true</code></pre>
				<p>For <code>SMTPS</code>, use <code>mail.smtps.socketFactory.fallback</code> in Java or
					<code>simplejavamail.extraproperties.mail.smtps.socketFactory.fallback</code> in external configuration.
					An explicit <code>false</code> keeps fallback disabled. This setting concerns your custom factory failing;
					it does not allow retrying in plaintext after a failed STARTTLS upgrade.</p>
			</div>
		</section>


		<a href="#section-whitelist-hosts" id="section-whitelist-hosts" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
		<h2>Certificate trust</h2>

		<section>
			<div class="view">
				<p>
				  If an internal relay uses your company's private CA, start by adding the CA to the JVM trust store. Simple Java Mail leaves <code class="inline language-properties">mail.*.ssl.trust</code> unset by default, so Angus checks the chain there.
				</p>
				<p>
				  Check two things when troubleshooting the relay. The certificate chain must lead to a trusted issuer, and the certificate must name the SMTP host you connected to.
				</p>
				<p>
				  If adding the private CA to the trust store is impractical, <code class="inline language-java">trustingSSLHosts(...)</code> limits the trust exception to named hosts. Reserve <code class="inline language-java">trustingAllHosts(true)</code> for last-resort compatibility work, since it accepts certificates from every host.
				</p>
				<p>
Conflicting explicit TLS settings in Java, raw properties, factory configuration or a supplied Session are rejected at construction.
Remove unwanted declarations at their source, or deliberately select the
<a href="/understanding-configuration.html#connection-conflicts">override policy</a>.
Configuration locks and mandatory encryption requirements apply in either mode.
</p>
			</div>
			<div class="side">
<pre><code>SimpleJavaMail.fromDefaults().mailerBuilder()
	.withSMTPServer("smtp.example.com", 587)
	.withTransportStrategy(TransportStrategy.SMTP_TLS)
	// secure default; shown explicitly here
	.withTlsConfig(TlsConfig.builder()
	    .trustingAllHosts(false)
	    .verifyingServerIdentity(true)
	    .build());

// narrow exception when the issuing CA cannot be added to the JVM trust store
SimpleJavaMail.fromDefaults().mailerBuilder().withSMTPServer("smtp.internal.example", 587)
	.withTlsConfig(TlsConfig.builder()
	    .trustingSSLHosts("smtp.internal.example")
	    .build());

// broad compatibility escape hatch; avoid in production
SimpleJavaMail.fromDefaults().mailerBuilder().withSMTPServer("smtp.internal.example", 587)
	.withTlsConfig(TlsConfig.builder()
	    .trustingAllHosts(true)
	    .build());
</code></pre>
				Or with property default:
<pre><code class="language-properties small">simplejavamail.defaults.trustallhosts=false
# optional narrow exception; ignored when trustallhosts is true:
simplejavamail.defaults.trustedhosts=smtp.internal.example</code></pre>
			</div>
		</section>


		<a href="#section-verify-server-identity" id="section-verify-server-identity" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
		<h2>Verifying server identity</h2>

		<section>
			<p class="wide">
				Simple Java Mail enables server identity verification for TLS connections by default (also see <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc2595.txt">RFC 2595</a>,
				2.4. Server Identity Check). Angus Mail checks that the certificate names the host used to start the connection.
			</p>
			<p class="wide">
				This does not replace certificate-chain validation. Keep both checks enabled for normal production use, including when a private CA has been added
				to the JVM trust store. Disabling hostname verification is intended only for controlled compatibility or testing scenarios.
			</p>
<pre><code>// enabled by default
SimpleJavaMail.fromDefaults().mailerBuilder().withSMTPServer("smtp.example.com", 587)
	.withTlsConfig(TlsConfig.builder()
	    .verifyingServerIdentity(true)
	    .build());

// compatibility escape hatch; avoid in production
SimpleJavaMail.fromDefaults().mailerBuilder().withSMTPServer("smtp.internal.example", 587)
	.withTlsConfig(TlsConfig.builder()
	    .verifyingServerIdentity(false)
	    .build());
</code></pre>
				Or with property default:
<pre><code class="language-properties small">simplejavamail.defaults.verifyserveridentity=true</code></pre>
		</section>


<section><p class="wide">An expired primary certificate can be a reason to use another approved relay, without trusting that expired certificate.
      <a href="/smtp-connection-failover.html#section-policy">Connection failover</a> provides separate opt-ins for authentication rejection and
      certificate verification failure. The selected destination still has to pass its own TLS checks, and mandatory message locks remain binding.</p></section>

		<a href="#section-crlf-scanning" id="section-crlf-scanning" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
		<h2>Scanning for suspicious content</h2>

		<section>
			<p class="wide">
Before sending, Simple Java Mail scans fields and headers for suspicious CR/LF sequences that could inject extra message headers.
</p>

			<p class="wide" style="margin-top: 1em">
				The values being scanned are:
			</p>
			<ul class="indent">
				<li>subject</li>
				<li>additional outgoing header names and values, including explicit choices</li>
				<li>every attachment name, content ID, nested datasource name and description</li>
				<li>every embedded image name, content ID, nested datasource name and description</li>
				<li>from recipient name and address</li>
				<li>every replyTo recipient name and address, if provided</li>
				<li>bounceTo recipient name and address, if provided</li>
				<li>every TO/CC/BCC recipient name and address</li>
				<li>disposition-notification-to recipient name and address, if provided</li>
				<li>return-receipt-to recipient name and address, if provided</li>
				<li><a href="/email-composition.html#section-dsn-envelope-id">fixed DSN envelope identifiers (ENVID)</a>, validated separately when their configuration is built</li>
			</ul>

			<p class="wide">
				Fixed envelope identifiers accept only printable ASCII, so CR, LF, tabs and other control characters are rejected before an Email can be built.
				The bundled Angus adapter encodes spaces, <code>+</code> and <code>=</code> just before sending, so an identifier cannot add another SMTP parameter.
				For example, <code>order RET=FULL</code> becomes <code>ENVID=order+20RET+3DFULL</code>, not a separate <code>RET</code> setting.
				Raw MAIL extensions and custom transport implementations remain caller-owned.
			</p>

			<p class="wide">
Background on header injection:
</p>
			<ul class="indent">
				<li><a href="https://web.archive.org/web/20160331233647/http://www.cakesolutions.net/teamblogs/2008/05/08/email-header-injection-security">Email Header Injection security</a></li>
				<li><a href="https://security.stackexchange.com/questions/54099/what-threats-come-from-crlf-in-email-generation/54100#54100">StackExchange - What threats come from CRLF in email generation?</a></li>
				<li><a href="https://archive.ph/NuETu">OWASP - Testing for IMAP SMTP Injection</a></li>
				<li><a href="https://archive.ph/uReuD">CWE-93: Improper Neutralization of CRLF Sequences ('CRLF Injection')</a></li>
			</ul>

			<p class="wide">
				Use the following setting to log ordinary client-validation findings, including header-scan findings, as warnings instead of blocking the send.
				The checks still run. ENVID validation and encoding are unaffected: an invalid fixed envelope identifier is still rejected.
			</p>
<pre><code>SimpleJavaMail.fromDefaults().mailerBuilder()
	.withValidationConfig(ValidationConfig.builder()
	    .withClientValidationWarningsOnly(true)
	    .build());
</code></pre>
		</section>


  <a href="#section-sending-dkim" id="section-sending-dkim" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  {{#> components/feature-heading-h2 dkim=true}}Signing emails with DKIM{{/components/feature-heading-h2}}
  <section><div class="wide full-width-example">
    <h3>Sign a receipt for your domain</h3>
    <p>For receipts from <code>receipts@example.org</code>, publish the public key for your selector in DNS. Give the DKIM module
      the matching private key, selector and signing domain. The receiver can then verify the signed parts.
      See <a href="https://www.endpointdev.com/blog/2014/04/spf-dkim-and-dmarc-brief-explanation/">how DKIM fits with SPF and DMARC</a>.</p>
<pre><code class="language-java">DkimConfig signing = DkimConfig.builder()
    .dkimPrivateKeyPath(Path.of("secrets/dkim-private-key.der"))
    .dkimSigningDomain("example.org")
    .dkimSelector("dkim1")
    .build();

Email receipt = mail.emailBuilder().startingBlank()
    .from("receipts@example.org").to("customer@example.net")
    .withSubject("Your receipt").withPlainText("Thank you for your order.")
    .signWithDomainKey(signing)
    .buildEmail();</code></pre>
    <p>Use the sender’s domain or a parent domain. Matching ignores domain case and preserves the supplied spelling.
      The default is RSA-SHA256 with relaxed header and body canonicalization. DKIM signs the final outgoing form, including any S/MIME or OpenPGP protection.</p>
    <h3>Sign every receipt from this Mailer</h3>
    <p>Put the same config on a defaults template. A message can supply its own DKIM config.</p>
<pre><code class="language-java">Email signingDefaults = mail.emailBuilder().startingBlank().signWithDomainKey(signing).buildEmail();
Mailer receiptMailer = mail.mailerBuilder().withEmailDefaults(signingDefaults).buildMailer();</code></pre>
    <p>The template can omit From. The prepared message needs a sender. Supplying a defaults template replaces property-derived
      message defaults. See <a href="configuration.html#section-config-mailer">defaults and overrides</a> to keep those values.</p>
<details class="doc-disclosure">
  <summary>Set the signing defaults in properties</summary>
<pre><code class="language-properties">simplejavamail.dkim.signing.private_key_file_or_data=file:my_dkim_key.der
simplejavamail.dkim.signing.selector=dkim1
simplejavamail.dkim.signing.signing_domain=example.org</code></pre>
      <p>Use <code>file:</code> for a path or <code>base64:</code> for inline key data. A missing explicit file or malformed Base64 fails
        during configuration and the error omits the key data. Unprefixed values are accepted for compatibility.
        <code>use_length_param</code> defaults to false, <code>header_canonicalization</code> and <code>body_canonicalization</code>
        to <code>RELAXED</code>, and <code>algorithm</code> to <code>SHA256_WITH_RSA</code>.</p>
</details>
<details class="doc-disclosure">
  <summary>If a downstream service appends a footer or rewrites a header</summary>
<p>Keep the default header set unless a known relay rewrites a specific header. Keep <code>From</code> signed. Excluding it fails
        before the module reads message content. Enabling the <code>l=</code> tag signs only the original body length and leaves appended content outside the signature.</p><pre><code class="small">DkimConfig.builder()
    .dkimPrivateKeyPath(Path.of("secrets/dkim-private-key.der"))
    .dkimSigningDomain("your-domain.org")
    .dkimSelector("dkim1")
    // Only if a known service appends a footer:
    .useLengthParam(true)
    // Only for headers that this relay rewrites:
    .excludedHeadersFromDkimDefaultSigningList("Message-ID", "Date")
    .build();
</code></pre>
</details>
<details class="doc-disclosure">
  <summary>Use an Ed25519 key</summary>
<p>Publish the matching public key under the selector and choose the Ed25519 signing algorithm.</p><pre><code class="language-java">DkimConfig signing = DkimConfig.builder()
    .dkimPrivateKeyPath(Path.of("secrets/dkim-ed25519-private-key.der"))
    .dkimSigningDomain("example.org")
    .dkimSelector("dkim-ed25519")
    .signingAlgorithm("SHA256_WITH_ED25519")
    .build();</code></pre>
</details>
<details class="doc-disclosure">
  <summary>Sign an existing MimeMessage</summary>
<p>Use the returned result. The helper finalizes and signs that message, which retains the signed bytes across repeated writes.</p><pre><code class="small">MimeMessage signed = MailerHelper.signMessageWithDKIM(
    mimeMessageToSign, emailContainingSigningDetails);
signed.writeTo(outputStream); // Or submit signed through your application-owned transport.</code></pre>
</details>
<details class="doc-disclosure">
  <summary>Load keys, handle failures and reset options</summary>
<ul>
      <li>Path, String path and File setters read and close the file immediately. <code>dkimPrivateKeyData(byte[])</code> copies the bytes.
        The data String overload encodes UTF-8 text without Base64 decoding. The stream overload reads immediately and you close the stream.</li>
      <li>Known key-file open, read and close failures use <code>MailException</code> with kind <code>PREPARATION</code>, retaining the cause and suppressed close errors.
        A replacement takes effect after reading, preparation and closing succeed. Key validation happens when signing.</li>
      <li>Built exclusions are immutable. Build a replacement to change them. <code>clearSigningAlgorithm()</code>,
        <code>clearHeaderCanonicalization()</code> and <code>clearExcludedHeadersFromDkimDefaultSigningList()</code> preserve the key and domain.
        A null DKIM domain or selector throws and preserves the previous value. A null optional algorithm selects the default.</li>
      <li>Builder getters can inspect unfinished choices. Keep key material and serialized configs private. Automatic rendering hides known secrets.</li>
      </ul>
</details>
  </div></section>

		<a href="#section-sending-smime" id="section-sending-smime" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
		{{#> components/feature-heading-h2 smime=true}}Signing / encrypting emails with S/MIME{{/components/feature-heading-h2}}
    <section>
      <div class="wide full-width-example">
        <h3 id="section-smime-sign-statement">Sign a statement</h3>
        <p>Use <a href="https://www.globalsign.com/en/blog/what-is-s-mime/">S/MIME</a> to sign a statement with your finance team's
          private key. Add <a href="/modules.html#smime-module"><code>smime-module</code></a> and supply the PKCS12 keystore,
          store password, signing key alias and key password.</p>
<pre><code class="language-java">SmimeSigningConfig signing = SmimeSigningConfig.builder()
    .pkcs12Config(Path.of("finance-signing.p12"), storePassword, keyAlias, keyPassword)
    .build();

EmailPopulatingBuilder statement = mail.emailBuilder().startingBlank()
    .from("finance@example.org")
    .to("reviewer@example.net")
    .withSubject("Monthly statement")
    .withPlainText(statementText);

mailer.sync().sendMail(statement.signWithSmime(signing).buildEmail());</code></pre>
        <h3 id="section-smime-encrypt-statement">Encrypt it for the reviewer</h3>
        <p>For a private statement, use this send in place of the signing-only example. Encrypt with the reviewer's public X.509
          certificate. Keep the signing config to sign first, then encrypt, or omit it to encrypt only. Protection is applied when
          Simple Java Mail prepares MIME output for conversion or sending.</p>
<pre><code class="language-java">SmimeEncryptionConfig encryption = SmimeEncryptionConfig.builder()
    .x509Certificate(Path.of("reviewer-certificate.pem"))
    .build();

mailer.sync().sendMail(statement.signWithSmime(signing)
    .encryptWithSmime(encryption)
    .buildEmail());</code></pre>
        <ul>
          <li>Give each reviewer their own certificate when they have one. A certificate on any To, Cc or Bcc recipient requests
            S/MIME encryption and takes precedence for that recipient. The Email or Mailer can supply a fallback for other recipients.</li>
          <li>Use one content-protection format per message. <code>buildEmail()</code> rejects S/MIME recipient certificates combined
            with <a href="#section-sending-openpgp">OpenPGP signing or encryption</a>.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    {{> components/pattern-examples topic="protected-content"}}
    <section>
      <div class="wide full-width-example">
        <details class="doc-disclosure">
          <summary>Set certificates for a group of recipients</summary>
          <p>Use <code>mail.recipientBuilder()</code> for individual certificates or <code>mail.recipientsBuilder()</code> to apply
            a certificate policy to a mailing list. Properties can supply one fallback certificate for the Email or Mailer.</p>
          <div class="doc-table-wrap" tabindex="0" aria-label="S/MIME recipient certificate options">
            <table><thead><tr><th>Recipient-group operation</th><th>Certificates on produced recipients</th></tr></thead><tbody>
              <tr><td><code>withDefaultSmimeCertificate(c)</code></td><td>Fill missing certificates only.</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>withFixedSmimeCertificate(c)</code></td><td>Replace every recipient certificate with <code>c</code>.</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>clearingSmimeCertificates()</code></td><td>Strip certificates, permitting the Email fallback.</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>resetSmimeCertificatePolicy()</code></td><td>Stop applying the group rule and keep the original certificates.</td></tr>
            </tbody></table>
          </div>
          <p>Resetting the policy leaves the source recipients, any already built list and all NOTIFY choices as they were.
            Email templates and separately added recipients can still request encryption after you clear a group's certificates.</p>
          <p>For two reviewers with individual certificates, configure the fallback for anyone without one.</p>
<pre><code>SmimeEncryptionConfig algorithmsAndFallback = SmimeEncryptionConfig.builder()
    .x509Certificate(fallbackCertificate)
    .keyEncapsulationAlgorithm("RSA_OAEP_SHA384")
    .cipherAlgorithm("AES256_CBC")
    .build();

Recipient alice = mail.recipientBuilder()
    .withAddress("alice@example.com")
    .withType(Message.RecipientType.TO)
    .withSmimeCertificate(aliceCertificate)
    .build();

Recipient bob = mail.recipientBuilder()
    .withAddress("bob@example.com")
    .withType(Message.RecipientType.TO)
    .withSmimeCertificate(bobCertificate)
    .build();

Email emailToBeEncrypted = currentEmailBuilder
    .withRecipients(alice, bob)
    .encryptWithSmime(algorithmsAndFallback)
    .buildEmail();
</code></pre>
          <p>To give a list the same certificate, use a fixed policy.</p>
<pre><code>List&lt;Recipient&gt; finance = SimpleJavaMail.fromDefaults().recipientsBuilder()
    .withFixedSmimeCertificate(financeCertificate)
    .withRecipientsWithDefaultName("Finance", Message.RecipientType.TO,
            "alice@example.com", "bob@example.com")
    .buildRecipients();

Email emailToBeEncrypted = currentEmailBuilder
    .withRecipients(finance)
    .buildEmail();
</code></pre>
          <p>To fill only missing certificates, use a default policy.</p>
<pre><code>List&lt;Recipient&gt; finance = SimpleJavaMail.fromDefaults().recipientsBuilder()
    .withDefaultSmimeCertificate(financeFallbackCertificate)
    .withRecipients(previousFinanceRecipients)
    .buildRecipients();
// recipients that already had their own certificates keep them;
// the others receive financeFallbackCertificate</code></pre>
          <p>To let the Email fallback apply to reused recipients, clear their certificates.</p>
<pre><code>List&lt;Recipient&gt; reusableRecipients = SimpleJavaMail.fromDefaults().recipientsBuilder()
    .clearingSmimeCertificates()
    .withRecipients(previousRecipients)
    .buildRecipients();

Email emailToBeEncrypted = currentEmailBuilder
    .withRecipients(reusableRecipients)
    .encryptWithSmime(emailLevelSmimeEncryptConfig)
    .buildEmail();
</code></pre>
          <p>To keep each reused recipient's original certificate after an earlier group policy, reset the policy.</p>
<pre><code class="language-java">IRecipientsBuilder group = mail.recipientsBuilder()
    .withRecipients(previousRecipients);

List&lt;Recipient&gt; cleared =
    group.clearingSmimeCertificates().buildRecipients();

List&lt;Recipient&gt; preserved =
    group.resetSmimeCertificatePolicy().buildRecipients();</code></pre>
        </details>
        <details class="doc-disclosure">
          <summary>Set Mailer defaults or clear a choice</summary>
          <ul>
            <li>Set S/MIME defaults on the Mailer or through properties to sign every message. Properties can select a single
              encryption fallback. Use Java recipient or group settings for different certificates.</li>
            <li><code>clearSmimeSigning()</code> and <code>clearSmimeEncryption()</code> clear their respective local choices.
              <code>clearSmime()</code> clears both. Recipient certificates and ordinary templates can still request encryption.
              Use per-field suppression to skip a template. Configuration locks still apply. See the
              <a href="understanding-configuration.html#configuration-precedence">scope and precedence table</a>.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>Set the signing default in Java.</p>
<pre><code class="language-java">Email protectionDefaults = mail.emailBuilder().startingBlank()
    .signWithSmime(signing)
    .buildEmailCompletedWithDefaultsAndOverrides(); // retain property defaults too

Mailer protectedMailer = mail.mailerBuilder()
    .withEmailDefaults(protectionDefaults)
    .buildMailer();</code></pre>
          <p>Or use properties for the signing identity and encryption fallback.</p>
<pre><code class="language-properties small"># Sign every email sent through a property-configured mailer.
simplejavamail.smime.signing.keystore=my_smime_keystore.pkcs12
simplejavamail.smime.signing.keystore_password=keystore_password
simplejavamail.smime.signing.key_alias=key_alias
simplejavamail.smime.signing.key_password=key_password
simplejavamail.smime.signing.algorithm=SHA256withRSA

# Encrypt with one fallback certificate and the selected algorithms.
# Recipient-level certificates configured in Java still take precedence.
simplejavamail.smime.encryption.certificate=shared-recipient-certificate.pem
simplejavamail.smime.encryption.key_encapsulation_algorithm=RSA
# AES is recommended; DES_EDE3_CBC remains available for legacy recipient compatibility.
simplejavamail.smime.encryption.cipher=AES256_CBC

# Optional when the application is fully property-driven.
simplejavamail.defaults.to.name=Finance
simplejavamail.defaults.to.address=alice@example.com,bob@example.com
</code></pre>
        </details>
        <details class="doc-disclosure">
          <summary>Keystore validation, algorithm options and transfer encoding</summary>
          <ul>
            <li><code>Pkcs12Config.build()</code> needs the store bytes, store password, key alias and key password. Use
              <code>""</code> or an empty <code>char[]</code> for an empty password. A missing field causes
              <code>IllegalArgumentException</code> naming the field. Getters can inspect an unfinished builder. The S/MIME
              operation checks store parsing, passwords and alias lookup.</li>
            <li><code>signWithSmime(...)</code>, <code>encryptWithSmime(...)</code>, <code>pkcs12Config(Pkcs12Config)</code>
              and <code>x509Certificate(X509Certificate)</code> reject null with <code>IllegalArgumentException</code> and
              keep the earlier choice. PKCS12 setters also preserve earlier values when passed null. The material setters retain
              the supplied configuration or certificate. Module loading and cryptographic work happen later.</li>
            <li><code>withSmimeCertificate(...)</code>, <code>withDefaultSmimeCertificate(...)</code> and
              <code>withFixedSmimeCertificate(...)</code> require a non-null certificate. Passing null throws
              <code>IllegalArgumentException</code> and keeps the current certificate or policy. Clear an individual certificate
              with <code>clearingSmimeCertificate()</code>, or use the group clear or reset operation.</li>
            <li>Choose the signature algorithm on <code>SmimeSigningConfig</code>. Choose key encapsulation and cipher on
              <code>SmimeEncryptionConfig</code>. Those classes list their supported algorithms. An optional algorithm set
              to null selects its documented module default. To restore those defaults explicitly, use <code>clearSignatureAlgorithm()</code>,
              <code>clearKeyEncapsulationAlgorithm()</code> or <code>clearCipherAlgorithm()</code>.</li>
            <li>S/MIME preserves prepared headers and the <a href="/email-composition.html#smime-body-transfer-encoding">selected
              body transfer encoding</a> for single-part and multipart messages. Before signing raw binary attachments, it
              selects a safe transfer encoding so MIME line canonicalization leaves the decoded bytes intact. The caller's
              content stays unchanged.</li>
          </ul>
        </details>
      </div>
    </section>


<section><div class="wide full-width-example">
      <h3 id="section-protect-existing-mime">Protect a MimeMessage you already have</h3>
      <p>If you’ve already edited a <code>MimeMessage</code>, pass it to the S/MIME helper. It takes the content and headers from
        that message and the protection settings from the <code>Email</code> you supply. Mailer defaults and overrides do not apply.</p>
<pre><code class="language-java">Email protection = mail.emailBuilder().startingBlank()
    .encryptWithSmime(SmimeEncryptionConfig.builder()
        .x509Certificate(readerCertificate).build())
    .buildEmail();

MimeMessage encrypted = MailerHelper.signAndOrEncryptMessageWithSmime(
    session, existingMimeMessage, protection);</code></pre>
      <h4 id="smime-helper-message-id">Choose the protected message’s Message-ID</h4>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Use a fresh ID.</strong> Leave the protection Email’s ID unset, as in the example. The helper generates an ID even if the supplied MIME message has one.</li>
        <li><strong>Keep an existing ID.</strong> The helper uses any nonempty ID on the protection Email, including an ID carried over by import or copying.
          To reuse the supplied MIME message’s ID, call <code>fixingMessageId(existingMimeMessage.getMessageID())</code> on the protection builder before building it.</li>
        <li><strong>Discard an inherited ID.</strong> Call <code>clearId()</code> on the copied builder to generate a fresh ID when protection runs.</li>
      </ul>
      <details class="doc-disclosure">
        <summary>Signing order, certificate selection and repeated writes</summary>
        <p>Add <code>smime-module</code> for signing, encryption, or both. Together they sign first and then encrypt.
          Recipient certificates, the Email’s fallback certificate and configured encryption algorithms follow the
          <a href="security.html#section-sending-smime">ordinary S/MIME rules</a>. The helper finalizes each protected result so repeated
          writes keep its content and ID. With neither operation requested, it returns the supplied message with its existing ID and needs no S/MIME module.</p>
      </details>

</div>
</section>

		<a href="#section-smime-key-cache" id="section-smime-key-cache" class="section-link h3">&sect;</a>
		<h3>Choose S/MIME key reuse capacity</h3>
		<section><div class="wide full-width-example">
      <p>Set <code>simplejavamail.smime.cache.capacity</code> to control how many loaded key configurations one factory keeps for
        signing and decryption. Each entry includes parsed keys and retained keystore and password material.</p>
      <table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Capacity</th><th scope="col">What the factory keeps</th></tr></thead><tbody>
        <tr><td><code>-1</code> (default)</td><td>All loaded entries, with no count limit.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>0</code></td><td>No completed entries. Overlapping operations can still share a load.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Positive integer</td><td>Up to that many completed entries. When full, the cache evicts the least recently used.</td></tr>
      </tbody></table>
      <p>For example, to keep up to 10,000 configurations:</p>
<pre><code class="language-java">SimpleJavaMail mail = SimpleJavaMail.withConfig(
    ConfigLoader.builder()
        .withMap(Map.of(ConfigKeys.SMIME_KEY_CACHE_CAPACITY.key(), 10_000))
        .load());</code></pre>
      <details class="doc-disclosure">
        <summary>Sharing keys, eviction and changing capacity</summary>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Share a factory.</strong> Its Mailers and converters share loaded keys. A second factory has its own cache, even with identical settings.
            A send uses the selected Mailer’s factory, including after failover. <code>SimpleJavaMail.fromDefaults()</code> and the static converters use the default factory.</li>
          <li><strong>Size the cache.</strong> Capacity counts configurations. Entries have no time-based expiry. Work in progress can finish using an evicted key,
            and later work can reload it. <code>ConfigLoader.DEFAULT_SMIME_KEY_CACHE_CAPACITY</code> supplies the default. Values below <code>-1</code>
            fail before key loading. Fractions, overflow and unsupported types are configuration errors.</li>
          <li><strong>Change the limit.</strong> Configure it at the factory root, or lock it with <code>simplejavamail.locked.smime.cache.capacity</code>.
            Named Mailers share that limit. To change it, create a replacement factory and use its Mailers and converters. Close old Mailers to release sending resources.
            Cached keys can live as long as you retain the factory or any participating Mailer or converter. There is no separate cache cleanup call.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Use the same factory to reuse its loaded keys for incoming decryption.</p>
<pre><code class="language-java">Email received = mail.converter().fromEml(incomingEml)
    .withSmimeDecryption(customerKeys)
    .toEmail();</code></pre>
      </details>
		</div></section>


        <a href="#section-reading-smime" id="section-reading-smime" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
        {{#> components/feature-heading-h2 smime=true}}Reading S/MIME messages and attachments{{/components/feature-heading-h2}}

        <section><div class="view">
            <p>
              Your partner sends you an encrypted PDF. Supply your recipient keys while converting their EML or Outlook MSG. When opening succeeds, read the body and attachment bytes through the normal getters. An attached message still occupies one attachment. Reading a getter starts no new input read, decryption or verification.
            </p>
<pre><code class="language-java">Email received = mail.converter()
    .fromEml(Path.of("partner-message.eml"))
    .withSmimeDecryption(recipientKeys)
    .toEmail();

String body = received.getPlainText();
AttachmentResource document = received.getAttachments().get(0);
byte[] usableDocument = document.readAllBytes();
Email attachedMessage = document.getEmail(); // Already parsed, or null.
</code></pre>
            <p>An independently protected PDF exposes the recovered PDF through that same resource when opening succeeds. Its complete protected input
                remains available separately through <code>received.getInspection().getOriginalAttachment(0)</code>. A file inside a whole-message
                encrypted envelope can have the same PDF as its original attachment; the enclosing envelope belongs to
                <code>getOriginalMessage()</code>. Names are not unique identifiers, and an attachment's position identifies the occurrence being inspected.</p>
            <p>When one valid configured key cannot open a particular file, readable neighbors remain available and the file keeps its protected payload
                with a source-linked Problem. Corrupt or unsupported input also keeps available evidence. An unusable configured keystore, an explicitly
                requested missing module, cancellation and unrelated application failures still fail the operation. Invalid signatures do not discard
                readable content. The <a href="#section-inspecting-received-protection">received-protection workflow</a> shows how an application can decide whether to act on it.</p>
        </div></section>

        <a href="#section-reading-smime-verification" id="section-reading-smime-verification" class="section-link h3">&sect;</a>
        {{#> components/feature-heading-h3 smime=true}}Read the protection of the value you use{{/components/feature-heading-h3}}
        <section><div class="wide full-width-example">
          <p>A partner sends an encrypted message with an invoice PDF. After opening the message, select the invoice and
            check its own protection. Use the same attachment index for the file and its inspection. If two files share a
            name, the index identifies the occurrence you picked.</p>
<pre><code class="language-java">int invoiceIndex = 0; // The invoice is the first attachment in this example.
AttachmentResource invoice = received.getAttachments().get(invoiceIndex);
ValueInspection invoiceProtection =
    received.getInspection().getAttachment(invoiceIndex);

EncryptionStatus encryption = invoiceProtection.getEncryptionStatus();
SignatureStatus signature = invoiceProtection.getSignatureStatus();</code></pre>
          <figure class="doc-diagram mermaid" data-pagefind-ignore>
%%{init: { "flowchart": { "padding": 8, "nodeSpacing": 20, "rankSpacing": 20, "wrappingWidth": 160 } } }%%
flowchart TB
    accTitle: Open the message, check the PDF, then approve the signer
    accDescr: A decryption key opens the outer S/MIME message. Inspect whether a verified signature fully covers the selected invoice PDF. The application still checks the observed signing key against the partner's approved key.
    open["&lt;b&gt;Open the message&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br/&gt;Decrypt the outer S/MIME layer"]
    pdf["&lt;b&gt;Check the invoice PDF&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br/&gt;Inspect its signature coverage"]
    signer["&lt;b&gt;Approve the signer&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br/&gt;Match the partner's signing key"]
    open --&gt; pdf --&gt; signer
          </figure>
          <p>Opening the outer message gives you readable content. For this invoice, require a signature that verifies and
            covers the whole PDF. Before acting on it, use the <a href="#section-inspecting-received-protection">received-protection
            workflow</a> to require a signing key your application approves. <code>ApprovedSigners</code> pins explicitly
            configured signing public keys. Certificate identity, validity, path discovery, revocation, organizational
            authorization and the claimed From address remain your application's responsibility.</p>
          <p>A reviewer reading HTML should inspect <code>getHTMLText()</code>. Plain text and Subject have their own
            <code>getPlainText()</code> and <code>getSubject()</code> inspections. Get the content itself from <code>Email</code>
            or <code>AttachmentResource</code>. For a value inside an attached message, start with
            <code>attachment.getEmail().getInspection()</code>.</p>
          <div class="doc-table-wrap" tabindex="0" aria-label="Signature status meanings">
  <table class="signature-status-table">
    <thead><tr><th>Status</th><th>Meaning for the selected value</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>VERIFIED</code></td><td>A verified signature fully covers this value. The signer still needs your application's approval.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>INVALID</code></td><td>A signature check failed.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>UNVERIFIED</code></td><td>A related signature was recognized, but verification was not established.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>UNSIGNED</code></td><td>The absence of a signature was established.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>UNKNOWN</code></td><td>Signature presence or coverage could not be established.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>MIXED</code></td><td>Outcomes differ, or only partial coverage is known. Read the individual observations.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>
          <details class="doc-disclosure">
            <summary>Inspect the S/MIME history and nested layers</summary>
            <p><code>received.getInspection().getSmime()</code> lists S/MIME signatures, decryption observations, algorithms,
              available signer-key details and source references. It keeps the recorded layers for this message's direct
              resources after replacements or clears. A child's internal layers stay with that child. Use the selected value
              for a coverage decision.</p>
          </details>
          <p id="nested-smime-ciphertext">Opening an outer layer establishes evidence for that layer. An unopened inner value
            still needs its own inspection. Missing or malformed MIME declarations keep their actual Problems and leave facts unknown.
            Conversion uses the supplied keys and the usual shared import limits.</p>
        </div></section>

        <a href="#section-reading-smime-merging" id="section-reading-smime-merging" class="section-link h3">&sect;</a>
        {{#> components/feature-heading-h3 smime=true}}One readable message, with retained originals{{/components/feature-heading-h3}}
        <section><div class="wide full-width-example">
          <h4>Read the message you opened</h4>
          <p>The invoice is in the attachments list. Use the same <code>Email</code> to read the selected body, Subject and
            reply addresses. The protected content supplies each value where available.</p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Body and Subject.</strong> A readable protected value wins, including an empty string. An already read
              native value can fill an absent or unavailable value. The Subject is null if neither is available.
              Plain text and HTML each have their own sources, derivation and protection evidence.</li>
            <li><strong>Headers and reply destinations.</strong> Recognized protected authored headers supply the addresses,
              identity, sent date and threading, including their omissions. Body-only protection leaves native headers in
              place. Generated adapter headers and attached messages cannot supply the parent's identity. If protected
              addresses are identified but unreadable, your application chooses the reply destinations. Available outer
              metadata keeps its own evidence.</li>
          </ul>
          <h4>Retrieve or forward the original you received</h4>
          <p>If a colleague needs the partner's original encrypted EML, retrieve the whole message. Use the same invoice index
            to retrieve the original attachment. The whole message holds the enclosing encryption layer. The original
            attachment may already have been a PDF. An independently encrypted attachment
            retains its own protected payload here.</p>
<pre><code class="language-java">EmailInspection inspection = received.getInspection();
AttachmentResource originalMessage = inspection.getOriginalMessage();
AttachmentResource originalInvoice = inspection.getOriginalAttachment(invoiceIndex);

if (originalMessage != null) {
    Email forward = mail.emailBuilder().forwardingOriginal(received)
        .from("operator@example.org")
        .to("reviewer@example.org")
        .buildEmail();
}</code></pre>
          <p>The forward carries the complete retained message as an attachment. The recipient still needs a decryption key.
            Check the <a href="#received-protection-reuse">reply and forward rules</a> when the new message includes other
            confidential content. If a complete original was unavailable, its getter returns null. Forwarding the original
            requires a complete capture.</p>
          <details class="doc-disclosure">
            <summary>Selection details for headers, images and calendars</summary>
            <ul>
              <li><code>getHeaders()</code> is a case-insensitive view of represented received and custom values. Read the
                retained original for the exact wire headers.</li>
              <li>HTML keeps its associated embedded resources. If a reference is missing, an unambiguous already read
                resource from the same message can fill it. Unused materialized images stay ordinary attachments.
                An ambiguous association keeps its source-linked Problem. An attached message keeps its own images.</li>
              <li>Calendar method and text are selected together.</li>
            </ul>
          </details>
          <details class="doc-disclosure">
            <summary>Original capture and edits</summary>
            <ul>
              <li>Conversion retains complete raw EML and MSG in the supplied format. A <code>MimeMessage</code> without
                captured raw input has no original file. An incomplete prefix is insufficient for original forwarding.
                For EML String input, the capture contains the UTF-8 bytes the converter actually consumed. Its external wire
                encoding remains unknown.</li>
              <li>Copies and snapshots can share captured storage. Originals and different decoded content can require
                additional memory. There is no automatic temporary-file spill or whole-JVM memory bound.</li>
              <li>Reading a child Email leaves its parent's attachment payload unchanged. To edit it, render the changed child
                and replace the parent attachment explicitly. Use <a href="/email-conversion.html#section-source-inspection">source
                inspection</a> for source data and Problems. Neither is added to outgoing MIME.</li>
            </ul>
<pre><code class="language-java">Email edited = mail.emailBuilder().copying(received)
    .withPlainText("Replacement body")
    .buildEmail();
// The new plain text is caller-supplied with unknown incoming protection.
// Unchanged fields keep their evidence; retained originals remain historical input.</code></pre>
            <p>After edits, check the current field or resource. The S/MIME history still describes the original sources.</p>
          </details>
        </div></section>

        <a href="#section-reading-smime-decryption" id="section-reading-smime-decryption" class="section-link h3">&sect;</a>
        {{#> components/feature-heading-h3 smime=true}}Supply incoming S/MIME keys{{/components/feature-heading-h3}}
        <section><div class="view">
<pre><code class="language-java">Pkcs12Config recipientKeys = Pkcs12Config.builder()
    .pkcs12Store(Path.of("recipient.p12"))
    .storePassword(storePassword)
    .keyAlias("recipient")
    .keyPassword(keyPassword)
    .build();

Email received = mail.converter()
    .fromOutlookMsg(Path.of("partner-message.msg"))
    .withSmimeDecryption(recipientKeys)
    .toEmail();
</code></pre>
            <p>Use <code>fromEml(...)</code> for EML input. Requesting decryption requires <code>smime-module</code>; ordinary recognition/import without that
                capability remains available. Key reuse belongs to the converter's configured factory, under its
                <a href="#section-smime-key-cache">S/MIME key reuse capacity</a>. A getter never loads keys or retries a failed attempt.</p>
            <p id="signed-parent-attachments">The supplied keys also apply to supported independent protected attachments in signed or OpenPGP content.
                Successful opening exposes their usable ordinary resources. Their observations remain associated with those resources; an encrypted
                attachment does not make every parent field decrypted.</p>
            <p>For a selected value, <code>DECRYPTED</code> means full established recovery from encrypted input; <code>ENCRYPTED</code> means its selected
                payload is recognized unopened ciphertext; <code>NOT_ENCRYPTED</code> needs established absence; <code>UNKNOWN</code> keeps unresolved
                coverage. Detailed attempts distinguish <code>DEFERRED</code>, <code>UNAVAILABLE</code> and <code>FAILED</code>. Decryption alone does not approve a sender.</p>
        </div></section>

        <a href="#section-inspecting-received-protection" id="section-inspecting-received-protection" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
        <h2>Check incoming mail before acting on it</h2>
        <section><div class="view">
            <p>
              Suppose your service automatically imports a partner's remittance PDF. Convert the email with your receive keys and select the attachment you intend to import. Before importing it, check that this exact PDF was recovered from encryption and carries a verified signature from a key you approve.
            </p>
<pre><code class="language-java">ApprovedSigners approvedPartner = ApprovedSigners.builder()
    .addSmimeCertificate(currentPartnerCertificate)
    .addSmimeCertificate(replacementPartnerCertificate)
    .build();

ProtectionRequirements documentRequirements = ProtectionRequirements.builder()
    .requireVerifiedSignatureFrom(approvedPartner)
    .requireDecryptedContent()
    .build();

Email received = mail.converter().fromEml(input)
    .withSmimeDecryption(recipientKeys)
    .toEmail();

int documentIndex = findRequiredDocument(received.getAttachments()); // Application choice.
AttachmentResource document = received.getAttachments().get(documentIndex);
ProtectionCheck check = documentRequirements.check(
    received.getInspection().getAttachment(documentIndex));

if (check.isSatisfied()) {
    processDocument(document.readAllBytes());
} else {
    holdForReview(check.getOutcome(), check.getReasons(), received.getProblems());
}
</code></pre>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>Pick the actual document.</strong> Your application supplies the lookup and decides whether to
                process or hold the file. Keep the attachment index, since names can repeat. If you need a value inside
                an attached message, use that child's inspection.</li>
              <li><strong>Check one qualifying signature.</strong> <code>requireVerifiedSignatureFrom(...)</code> needs
                the same signature to verify, fully cover the selected value and use an approved signing key. An invalid
                approved signature cannot combine with a valid unapproved one. A renewed S/MIME certificate can match if
                the normalized public key is the same. Approve a new key explicitly. Your application still owns
                certificate validity and trust decisions.</li>
              <li><strong>Approve the OpenPGP signing key.</strong> Use
                <code>ApprovedSigners.builder().addOpenPgpSigningKeyFingerprint(fullFingerprint).build()</code> with the
                full fingerprint of the actual signing key, including the signing subkey when applicable. A short ID or
                approval of a different key is insufficient. Supplying a verification key enables verification, while
                approval is a separate choice.</li>
              <li><strong>Check decryption where you need it.</strong> <code>requireDecryptedContent()</code> checks
                recorded evidence that this value was recovered from encryption. A plain PDF or unopened encrypted
                payload fails the requirement. Run the check on each value you will use, whether that is the HTML,
                Subject or one attachment.</li>
            </ul>
            <div class="doc-table-wrap" tabindex="0" aria-label="Protection check outcomes">
                <table><thead><tr><th>Outcome</th><th>Application meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                    <tr><td><code>SATISFIED</code></td><td>The supplied requirements are established for this selected value.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>NOT_SATISFIED</code></td><td>At least one requirement is positively unmet, such as an unsigned document or a definitely unapproved signing key.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>INDETERMINATE</code></td><td>Unavailable evidence prevents a decision, such as an unverified candidate that might otherwise qualify.</td></tr>
                </tbody></table>
            </div>
            <p>Reasons have stable codes, explanations and captured source references. They belong to the returned check and do not add Email Problems.
                A recognized unsigned message can have no processing Problems while failing a required-signature rule. A missing value differs from a
                present empty value. Deferred, unavailable and uncaptured history do not become successful empty checks.</p>
            <h3 id="received-protection-explanations">Explain the selected value</h3>
<pre><code class="language-java">ValueInspection html = received.getInspection().getHTMLText();
html.getOrigin();
html.getDerivation();
html.getAvailability();
html.getSignatureStatus();
html.getEncryptionStatus();

for (SignatureInspection signature : html.getSignatures()) {
    ProtectionCoverage coverage = signature.getCoverage();
    SignatureObservation observation = signature.getObservation();
    SignatureVerification verification = observation.getStatus();
    String signingKey = observation.getSigningKeyFingerprint();
    // Explain this observation and its captured sources without verifying again.
}
</code></pre>
            <p>A wholly signed and encrypted HTML representation can have <code>VERIFIED</code>, <code>DECRYPTED</code> and full coverage. If protected
                content supplies only plain text while the native message supplies HTML, both remain readable; checking the plain text cannot approve
                that fallback HTML. Its own inspection describes its native/derived origin and actual coverage. Native origin alone is not proof of
                unsigned content: an enclosing layer may itself cover it.</p>
            <p>
A fully covering, verified, approved signature can satisfy the base requirement even when another signature is invalid
and the summary is <code>MIXED</code>. Add <code>requireAllSignaturesVerified()</code> when every relevant signature must verify;
keep the approved-signature requirement as well. Known partial coverage remains <code>PARTIAL</code> and cannot satisfy full coverage.
Unknown coverage remains uncertain. Each check applies to one selected value.
</p>
            <p>Keep the complete original input when your application needs evidence or later original-preserving forwarding. Use
                <code>received.getInspection().getOriginalMessage()</code> and <code>getOriginalAttachment(documentIndex)</code>; null means no suitable
                complete captured input was retained. Do not rebuild a signed archive from getters and call it the original. Protect your stored plaintext,
                original input and credentials under your own retention/access policy.</p>
            <h3 id="received-protection-reuse">Reply or forward deliberately</h3>
<pre><code class="language-java">Email interpretedForward = mail.emailBuilder().forwarding(received)
    .from("operator@example.org").to("reviewer@example.org")
    .encryptWithSmime(reviewerEncryption)
    .buildEmail();

Email originalForward = mail.emailBuilder().forwardingOriginal(received)
    .from("operator@example.org").to("reviewer@example.org")
    .buildEmail();
</code></pre>
            <p>
              A reviewer can receive the recovered PDF with the edits you made, or the original message for inspection. <code>forwarding(received)</code> uses the represented values. <code>forwardingOriginal(received)</code> attaches the complete retained EML or MSG as supplied and reports a failure if a suitable original is unavailable. Reading a parsed child changes neither payload. An intact encrypted original can travel in an unencrypted outer message when the rest of that message reveals no confidential content. The reviewer still needs their own decryption key.
            </p>
            <p>A child's retained original contains that child's EML or MSG, without its parent's encryption envelope. If it was extracted through the
                parent's encryption, forwarding that original still reuses confidential content and needs outgoing encryption or the conversion's explicit
                permission. Even an encrypted child body can leave its own headers clear inside that original file. A signature alone does not establish confidentiality.</p>
            <p>For known confidential input, the new original-forward carrier uses a neutral format-based filename and omits inherited description/CID
                metadata. The original resource getter keeps its actual input metadata and bytes.</p>
            <p>
              If you quote decrypted text from the partner's message in a reply, configure encryption for the reviewer. You can read and edit the draft. Simple Java Mail checks automatically reused confidential content at final MIME or EML output and sending, after outgoing settings are resolved. Suitable enclosing encryption can satisfy that check. Configure outgoing protection for the draft separately from the keys you used to read the original.
            </p>
<pre><code class="language-java">Email permittedReply = mail.emailBuilder()
    .replyingTo(received, ReplyOptions.allowUnencryptedContent())
    .from("operator@example.org")
    .buildEmail();

byte[] handoff = mail.converter().fromEmail(confidentialDraft)
    .allowUnencryptedContent()
    .toEmlBytes();
// This conversion's permission leaves confidentialDraft unchanged.
// A fresh conversion or later send still needs protection or draft permission.
</code></pre>
            <p><code>ForwardOptions.allowUnencryptedContent()</code> supplies the corresponding deliberate forward permission. Neither permission disables
                configured or mandatory outgoing encryption or allows fallback when encryption fails. Ordinary copies/snapshots preserve a draft's own
                choice; a new reply or forward uses its own options. Editing one body does not waive a remaining quote. Explicitly clearing every reused
                confidential body/resource removes those components' requirement; merely replacing text does not prove the quote is gone.</p>
            <p>The received getter keeps a private Subject. A new helper-generated public Subject uses an actually known public counterpart, otherwise
                <code>Re: Your message</code> or <code>Fwd: Message</code>. Permission to reuse body content leaves that safe default in place. Body
                encryption does not hide every outer header; this is not full outbound header protection. See
                <a href="/email-composition.html#section-reply-forward">reply/forward details</a>, the
                <a href="email-workload-field-guide.html#check-a-partner-document-before-processing-it">partner-document recipe</a> and the
                <a href="patterns/protected-partners.html">runnable protected-partners application</a>.</p>
        </div></section>

		<a href="#section-sending-openpgp" id="section-sending-openpgp" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
		{{#> components/feature-heading-h2 openpgp=true}}Signing / encrypting emails with OpenPGP/MIME{{/components/feature-heading-h2}}

    <section>
      <div class="wide full-width-example">
        <h3 id="section-openpgp-sign-export">Sign an export</h3>
        <p>Add <a href="/modules.html#openpgp-module"><code>openpgp-module</code></a> for <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc3156.html">RFC 3156</a>
          signing and encryption. For a signed daily export, supply your secret signing key ring and its passphrase.
          Use armored or binary key-ring bytes from your own key store.</p>
<pre><code class="language-java">OpenPgpSigningConfig signing = OpenPgpSigningConfig.builder()
    .secretKeyRing(senderSecretKeyRing)
    .passphrase(senderPassphrase)
    .build();

EmailPopulatingBuilder export = mail.emailBuilder().startingBlank()
    .from("reports@example.org")
    .to("alice@example.net")
    .withSubject("Daily export")
    .withPlainText(exportText);

mailer.sync().sendMail(export.signWithOpenPgp(signing).buildEmail());</code></pre>
        <h3 id="section-openpgp-encrypt-export">Encrypt it for your partner</h3>
        <p>For a private export, use this send in place of the signing-only example. Supply the partner's public key ring.
          Keep the signing config to sign the clear MIME entity first and then encrypt it, or omit it to encrypt only.</p>
<pre><code class="language-java">OpenPgpEncryptionConfig encryption = OpenPgpEncryptionConfig.builder()
    .addRecipientPublicKeyRing(alicePublicKeyRing)
    .build();

mailer.sync().sendMail(export.signWithOpenPgp(signing)
    .encryptWithOpenPgp(encryption)
    .buildEmail());</code></pre>
        <ul>
          <li>Add one public key ring per key that should be able to decrypt the export. The key list is separate from
            To, Cc and Bcc. Your application chooses and distributes the keys. The module performs no WKD or public
            keyserver lookup.</li>
          <li>Use either S/MIME or OpenPGP/MIME on a message. An S/MIME certificate on a recipient also requests S/MIME
            encryption. DKIM works with either format and signs the final outgoing message after content protection.</li>
          <li>Protect stored snapshots. Email serialization retains your secret signing keys and passphrases. Encryption-only
            configurations store the recipient public key rings and cipher choice. See
            <a href="/email-conversion.html#section-serializing-emails">Serializing Email objects</a>.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <div class="wide full-width-example">
        <details class="doc-disclosure">
          <summary>Install the Maven dependency</summary>
<pre><code class="language-xml">&lt;dependency&gt;
    &lt;groupId&gt;org.simplejavamail&lt;/groupId&gt;
    &lt;artifactId&gt;openpgp-module&lt;/artifactId&gt;
    &lt;version&gt;{{site.version}}&lt;/version&gt;
&lt;/dependency&gt;</code></pre>
        </details>
        <details class="doc-disclosure">
          <summary>Convert an S/MIME draft to OpenPGP/MIME</summary>
          <p>OpenPGP signing or encryption conflicts with S/MIME on the Email or with any recipient certificate,
            including certificates from group defaults or fixed policies. <code>buildEmail()</code> throws
            <code>IllegalArgumentException</code> identifying the conflicting choices. This check uses metadata and needs no
            module load, key inspection or content read.</p>
          <p>Completion checks again after applying defaults, overrides and mandatory locks. A conflict from the selected
            Mailer's policy fails before MIME protection or submission. Use <code>clearSmime()</code> for Email-level
            settings. For reused recipients, remove S/MIME certificates from copies and replace the copied Email's recipients.
            The source Email and recipients keep their original settings. Template suppression applies to ordinary settings.
            Locks still apply.</p>
<pre><code class="language-java">List&lt;Recipient&gt; pgpRecipients = mail.recipientsBuilder()
    .withRecipients(smimeEmail.getRecipients())
    .clearingSmimeCertificates()
    .buildRecipients();

Email pgpEmail = mail.emailBuilder().copying(smimeEmail)
    .clearSmime()
    .clearRecipients()
    .withRecipients(pgpRecipients)
    .signWithOpenPgp(signing)
    .buildEmail();</code></pre>
        </details>
        <details class="doc-disclosure">
          <summary>Set defaults or clear a choice</summary>
          <ul>
            <li>Use <code>withEmailDefaults(...)</code> or <code>withEmailOverrides(...)</code> with an Email template.
              Each signing or encryption configuration is one complete choice. An override wins over an Email's choice,
              which wins over a default. Encryption key-ring lists stay separate.</li>
            <li><code>clearOpenPgpSigning()</code>, <code>clearOpenPgpEncryption()</code> and <code>clearOpenPgp()</code>
              allow template fallback. Suppress <code>OPENPGP_SIGNING_CONFIG</code> or
              <code>OPENPGP_ENCRYPTION_CONFIG</code> to skip a default or override. Exact EML keeps its bytes, including
              across Java serialization.</li>
          </ul>
<pre><code class="language-java">Email protectionDefaults = mail.emailBuilder().startingBlank()
    .signWithOpenPgp(signing)
    .encryptWithOpenPgp(encryption)
    .buildEmail();

Mailer protectedMailer = mail.mailerBuilder()
    .withEmailDefaults(protectionDefaults)
    .buildMailer();</code></pre>
        </details>
        <details class="doc-disclosure">
          <summary>Choose a signing key or algorithm</summary>
          <div class="doc-table-wrap" tabindex="0" aria-label="OpenPGP key and algorithm choices">
            <table><thead><tr><th>Choice</th><th>Default</th><th>Options</th><th>Reset</th></tr></thead><tbody>
              <tr><td>Signing key</td><td>Automatic</td><td>Use <code>signingKeyId(...)</code> if the secret ring contains
                more than one signing key.</td><td><code>clearSigningKeyId()</code></td></tr>
              <tr><td>Signature hash</td><td>SHA-256</td><td>SHA-384, SHA-512</td><td><code>resetHashAlgorithm()</code></td></tr>
              <tr><td>Encryption cipher</td><td>AES-256</td><td>AES-128, AES-192</td><td><code>resetSymmetricAlgorithm()</code></td></tr>
            </tbody></table>
          </div>
          <p>Resets leave keys and passphrases on the builder. Built configurations keep their original choices. They copy key-ring
            bytes and passphrases and hide them from <code>toString()</code>. Serialization retains the chosen signing key,
            hash and cipher.</p>
<pre><code class="language-java">OpenPgpSigningConfig signing = OpenPgpSigningConfig.builder()
    .secretKeyRing(senderSecretKeyRing)
    .passphrase(senderPassphrase)
    .signingKeyId(signingKeyId) // optional
    .hashAlgorithm(OpenPgpSigningConfig.HashAlgorithm.SHA512)
    .build();

OpenPgpEncryptionConfig encryption = OpenPgpEncryptionConfig.builder()
    .addRecipientPublicKeyRing(recipientPublicKeyRing)
    .symmetricAlgorithm(
        OpenPgpEncryptionConfig.SymmetricAlgorithm.AES_256)
    .build();</code></pre>
        </details>
      </div>
    </section>


		<a href="#section-openpgp-key-replacement" id="section-openpgp-key-replacement" class="section-link h3">&sect;</a>
		{{#> components/feature-heading-h3 openpgp=true}}Replacing recipient keys{{/components/feature-heading-h3}}

		<section>
			<div class="view">
				<ul>
				  <li><strong>Add one recipient.</strong> Use <code>addRecipientPublicKeyRing(...)</code> to append a key ring.</li>
				  <li><strong>Rotate or replace the list.</strong> Use <code>recipientPublicKeyRings(...)</code> to replace all keys
				    and keep your cipher setting. It copies the list and its byte arrays. A null list or entry throws and keeps the
				    previous selection. Pass an empty list or use <code>clearRecipientPublicKeyRings()</code> to clear the
				    selection.</li>
				  <li><strong>Check what you picked.</strong> <code>getRecipientPublicKeyRings()</code> returns an editable copy.
				    The builder accepts non-null empty byte arrays while you make your choices. <code>build()</code> requires at
				    least one ring and rejects empty rings. The module parses the keys when it applies protection. Earlier built
				    configurations keep their own keys.</li>
				</ul>
			</div>
			<div class="side">
<pre><code class="language-java">var recipientKeys = OpenPgpEncryptionConfig.builder()
    .addRecipientPublicKeyRing(alicePublicKeyRing)
    .symmetricAlgorithm(
        OpenPgpEncryptionConfig.SymmetricAlgorithm.AES_128);

recipientKeys.recipientPublicKeyRings(
    java.util.Arrays.asList(alicePublicKeyRing, bobPublicKeyRing));

// Inspect detached choices before building.
java.util.List&lt;byte[]&gt; selectedKeys =
    recipientKeys.getRecipientPublicKeyRings();
OpenPgpEncryptionConfig encryption = recipientKeys.build();</code></pre>
			</div>
		</section>


        <a href="#section-reading-openpgp" id="section-reading-openpgp" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
        {{#> components/feature-heading-h2 openpgp=true}}Reading OpenPGP/MIME messages{{/components/feature-heading-h2}}
        <section><div class="view">
            <p>
              When a partner's OpenPGP message arrives as an EML file, pass the file and your receive keys to the converter. The same configuration works with a supplied <code>MimeMessage</code>. Your app fetches the mail and chooses which signing keys to approve. The receive configuration supplies keys for verification and decryption.
            </p>
<pre><code class="language-java">OpenPgpReceiveConfig receive = OpenPgpReceiveConfig.builder()
    .addVerificationKeyRing(senderPublicKeyRing)
    .addDecryptionKeyRing(recipientSecretKeyRing, recipientPassphrase)
    .build();

Email received = mail.converter().fromEml(emlInputStream)
    .withOpenPgpReceiveConfig(receive)
    .toEmail();

ProtectionInspection openPgp = received.getInspection().getOpenPgp();
ValueInspection document = received.getInspection().getAttachment(0);
</code></pre>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>Read what opened.</strong> Use the normal getters for the selected bodies and attachment
                bytes. An attached message has its own Email and inspection. If you also receive S/MIME, supply
                <code>withSmimeDecryption(...)</code>. Install the corresponding optional module for each format. Nested
                processing shares the import's limits.</li>
              <li><strong>Verify the received MIME.</strong> Import decodes Base64 or quoted-printable transfer encoding
                on the signature or ciphertext, then verifies the received signed MIME entity. An empty MIME header area
                keeps its default plain-text meaning. Exact EML relay still requires canonical bytes even when ordinary
                import can read other line endings.</li>
              <li><strong>Look up a passphrase if needed.</strong> Use <code>passphraseProvider(...)</code> to resolve
                passphrases by key ID. The callback runs only if processing needs it. Return null to use the key ring's
                configured passphrase. If the callback fails, inspect the original cause. Missing keys and malformed
                inputs produce separate observations.</li>
            </ul>
            <p id="receive-key-draft-validation">
Receive-key setters copy non-null key material. You can inspect unfinished empty bytes on the builder,
but <code>build()</code> rejects empty keys. Getters return separate copies without invoking the passphrase provider;
the OpenPGP module interprets the keys during protected-mail processing.
Use the <a href="#section-inspecting-received-protection">requirements workflow</a> to approve the full fingerprint of an actual signing key.
</p>
        </div></section>

        <a href="#section-reading-openpgp-status" id="section-reading-openpgp-status" class="section-link h3">&sect;</a>
        {{#> components/feature-heading-h3 openpgp=true}}Inspect OpenPGP observations{{/components/feature-heading-h3}}
        <section><div class="view">
            <p>
Use <code>getOpenPgp()</code> for the immutable signature/decryption history and its completion status.
Each signature records the actual signing-key fingerprint, available key ID, algorithms, verification result, explanation and source references;
repeated signatures from the same key remain separate. Direct resources keep their recorded opening layers after edits,
while an attached child's internal body inspection stays on that child. Check the selected value before trusting its content.
</p>
<pre><code class="language-java">for (SignatureObservation signature : received.getInspection().getOpenPgp().getSignatures()) {
    SignatureVerification verification = signature.getStatus();
    String signingKeyFingerprint = signature.getSigningKeyFingerprint();
    String algorithm = signature.getSignatureAlgorithm();
    // Diagnostic facts; application approval remains separate.
}

ProtectionCheck decision = documentRequirements.check(
    received.getInspection().getAttachment(0));
</code></pre>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>A key is missing.</strong> A recognized signature without a verification key reports
                <code>UNVERIFIED</code>. When signature presence or coverage cannot be established, it remains
                <code>UNKNOWN</code>. Use the same <a href="#section-reading-smime-verification">signature and
                encryption meanings</a> as for S/MIME.</li>
              <li><strong>The wrapper is inconsistent.</strong> An unencrypted packet stays unencrypted even if its MIME
                wrapper says otherwise. Malformed MIME declarations and mismatched protocols can leave facts unknown.
                Read the available sources and Problems. Decryption evidence and individual signature results remain
                available for the layers the library processed.</li>
              <li><strong>The module is missing.</strong> Without <code>openpgp-module</code>, ordinary import keeps
                available metadata and recognizable protected input. It leaves unestablished results unknown or
                unavailable. Supplying an explicit receive configuration requires the module.</li>
              <li><strong>You need the original bytes.</strong> <a href="/email-conversion.html#section-exact-eml">Exact
                EML construction</a> keeps the authoritative bytes and a separate SMTP envelope. It records available
                protection facts and leaves facts unknown when support or keys are missing. Use ordinary conversion to
                open and read the content with your receive keys. Inspection never changes exact bytes.</li>
              <li><strong>You store the imported message.</strong> Complete originals stay in their actual format and
                can be shared by copies. Different decoded content and originals take additional memory, so import byte
                limits cover only part of the memory used. Deserializing an Email requires the classes and modules in
                its object graph. It cannot recover missing protection evidence.</li>
            </ul>
        </div></section>
